"L421@P9: 2.2.2. Blow up sequences. A price one has to pay for using a finer notion of blow ups\nL422@P9: is that the composition X′′ → X′ → X of blow ups cannot be considered as a blow\nL423@P9: up in a natural way: though X′′ → X is isomorphic to a blow up BlW (X) → X, it\nL424@P9: is not clear how to choose W canonically. Therefore we define a blow up sequence\nL425@P9: of length n to be a composition of n ≥ 0 blow ups Xn\nL426@P9: fn→ · · ·f2→ X1f1→ X0 with\nL427@P9: centers Vi֒→ Xi. In particular, a blow up sequence of length 0 is just the object\nL428@P9: X0 itself. Such sequence is called empty. It will be convenient to denote a blow up\nL429@P9: sequence as f : Xn 99K X0 and say that Vi’s are its centers. We stress that a blow\nL430@P9: up sequence is not defined by the morphism Xn → X even when we blow up regular\nL431@P9: schemes along regular centers, see [Kol, 3.33]. A sequence will be called contracted\nL432@P9: if all its blow ups are non-empty. Using dashed arrows we will sometimes split blow\nL433@P9: up sequences, e.g. f : Xn 99K Xi+1 → Xi 99K X0."
